<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Fixes</a> / Is my SSD failing?</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Storage troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">How to tell if your SSD is failing.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">If your SSD might be failing, back up first, then check its health with free Windows tools and the manufacturer’s utility. This guide shows you what symptoms mean, which readouts matter, and when to replace the drive.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Copy your important files somewhere else first. Then check the drive’s health in Windows 11 (<strong>Settings → System → Storage → Advanced storage settings → Disks &amp; volumes → Properties</strong>), or in the SSD maker’s free tool. Look for errors, a critical warning, or very low remaining life. If you see any of those, plus symptoms like freezes or corrupted files, replace the drive.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Symptoms</p><h2>Signs worth taking seriously.</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Strong signs</strong><p>Files that won’t open or come back corrupted, Windows making the drive read-only, the drive vanishing until a restart, or repeated crashes while saving.</p></div>
<div><strong>Possible signs</strong><p>Freezes of several seconds when opening files, blue screens that mention storage, and long boot times that started suddenly.</p></div>
<div><strong>Usually something else</strong><p>General slowness on a nearly full drive, or a drive that runs hot during long copies. Free up space and check temperatures before blaming the hardware.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Back up before you test.</h2>
<p>Any check that reads the whole drive puts load on it. If the drive is genuinely failing, that load can be the last thing it does. Copy the files you can’t lose to an external drive or cloud storage first. The <a href="/guides/back-up-windows-pc-before-replacing-drive/">backup guide</a> covers the free options, and a drive such as the <a href="/gear/budget-tech/wd-my-passport-4tb/">WD My Passport 4TB</a> gives you room to copy files. If you are removing a bare SATA or NVMe SSD, a <a href="/gear/budget-tech/sabrent-usb-c-lay-flat-drive-dock-nvme-sata/">USB-C lay-flat drive dock</a> makes connecting it much easier.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Check the health readout in Windows 11.</h2>
<p>Go to <strong>Settings → System → Storage → Advanced storage settings → Disks &amp; volumes</strong>, choose the drive, and select <strong>Properties</strong>. For supported NVMe SSDs, Windows shows a <strong>Drive health</strong> section with estimated remaining life, available spare, and temperature. A warning such as “Reliability is degraded” means the drive has reported serious errors.</p>
<p>SATA SSDs usually don’t show this section, so use the next two steps instead.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Read the reliability counters in PowerShell.</h2>
<p>Open PowerShell as administrator and run:</p>
<pre><code>Get-PhysicalDisk | Get-StorageReliabilityCounter | Format-List DeviceId, Wear, Temperature, ReadErrorsUncorrected, WriteErrorsUncorrected, PowerOnHours</code></pre>
<p>This only reads information. Not every drive reports every field, so blanks are normal. What matters is <strong>uncorrected</strong> read or write errors above zero, and a <strong>Wear</strong> figure that is high and climbing.</p>
<p>To match a <code>DeviceId</code> to a drive name, run <code>Get-PhysicalDisk</code> on its own.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Use the SSD maker’s tool.</h2>
<p>Most SSD brands provide a free utility, such as Samsung Magician, Crucial Storage Executive, or WD Dashboard. These read the drive’s own health data, show warnings in plain language, and install firmware updates, which sometimes fix stability bugs. Download it only from the manufacturer’s site.</p>
<p>CrystalDiskInfo is a free alternative that works with most brands and shows the raw SMART data.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Check Event Viewer for disk errors.</h2>
<p>Open Event Viewer, go to <strong>Windows Logs → System</strong>, and filter for errors and warnings. Repeated entries from disk or storage sources, such as reports of bad blocks or a device reset, that line up with your freezes or crashes support a hardware problem.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Keep it, watch it, or replace it?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Keep it</strong><p>Health is good, there are no uncorrected errors, and the symptoms have another explanation, like a full drive.</p></div>
<div><strong>Watch it</strong><p>Remaining life is falling faster than you expect, or there are a few corrected errors. Keep backups current and check again monthly.</p></div>
<div><strong>Replace it</strong><p>A critical warning, uncorrected errors, read-only mode, or corrupted files. Back up now and plan the swap.</p></div>
</div>
<p>If the drive is under warranty, the maker’s tool usually shows the serial number you need for a claim. Back up first, because warranty replacements don’t include your data.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">How this guide was made</p><p>These checks follow Microsoft’s PowerShell and Windows Storage documentation, plus the SMART/health reporting standards used by SSD makers including WD. StackGeist has not tested a failing SSD for this guide.</p></section>

